**(b) ‖dt·v‖ (one model update) as the default knob; ‖z0‖ as a cheap secondary readout.**
The 48→512→2048 sprawl + the dead zone come from the head-gradient magnitude varying wildly
per head/feature, un-normalized. Normalizing the guidance step to the model's OWN per-step
update ‖dt·v‖ expresses gain in the sampler's natural units — "gain 1 = one denoising step's
worth of push" — trajectory-aware (velocity magnitude changes over the schedule) and it
dissolves the per-head sprawl in a single move (every head renormalized to model-update units).
It's also the modern normalized-guidance convention. ‖z0‖ (fraction-of-latent-moved) is the
more *perceptual* "how much did it change" number a musician might also want on screen, and it's
cheap — so the job's "calibrate both" instinct was right: keep ‖z0‖ as a readout, but the KNOB
is ‖dt·v‖. **Caveat:** this is the GUIDANCE-path gain (TFG/LatCH); the `sa3_control` ADAPTER
gain (~6, forward FiLM) is a separate mechanism this normalization doesn't touch — don't
conflate them. Since it's a product-knob decision, I'm putting the ‖z0‖-vs-‖dt·v‖ call to Kim
in parallel; my rec stands but he owns the knob.

